INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'pgcil-engineer-trainee-319-posts-20261103', 'global', 'India',
'PGCIL Engineer Trainee Recruitment 2026: 319 Posts Through GATE 2026, Apply by 18 November',
'',
'POWERGRID opened applications on 3 November 2026 for 319 Engineer Trainee posts through GATE 2026, with Electrical taking 250 posts, Civil 48 and Computer Science 21; the window closes on 18 November 2026 at 11:59 PM.',
'',
$$<p>PGCIL Engineer Trainee recruitment 2026 opened on 3 November 2026, when Power Grid Corporation of India Limited (POWERGRID) released its vacancy notice for 319 Engineer Trainee posts to be filled through GATE 2026. The notice, dated 3 November 2026 under Advertisement No. CC/04/2025, gives engineering graduates a two-week window: online applications and fee payment run from 3 November 2026 at 5:00 PM to 18 November 2026 at 11:59 PM. POWERGRID, the company said, is a Maharatna public sector undertaking under the Ministry of Power, and trainees will be posted across India.</p>
<h2>At a glance</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Organisation</th><td>Power Grid Corporation of India Limited (POWERGRID), a Maharatna PSU under the Ministry of Power</td></tr>
<tr><th>Post</th><td>Engineer Trainee (ET-2026)</td></tr>
<tr><th>Advertisement number</th><td>CC/04/2025, dated 27 September 2025; vacancy notice dated 3 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><th>Total vacancies</th><td>319</td></tr>
<tr><th>Application window</th><td>3 November 2026, 5:00 PM to 18 November 2026, 11:59 PM</td></tr>
<tr><th>Recruitment route</th><td>GATE 2026 score only, followed by Group Discussion and Personal Interview</td></tr>
<tr><th>Pay during training</th><td>Rs. 40,000 to Rs. 1,40,000 (IDA), with an approximate CTC of Rs. 11 lakh a year</td></tr>
<tr><th>Pay after training</th><td>Rs. 50,000 to Rs. 1,60,000 (IDA) as Engineer (E2), with an approximate CTC of Rs. 22.5 lakh a year</td></tr>
<tr><th>Application fee</th><td>Rs. 500 for General, OBC (NCL) and EWS candidates; exempted for SC, ST, PwBD, Ex-servicemen and departmental candidates</td></tr>
<tr><th>How to apply</th><td>Online through the careers section of powergrid.in</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>PGCIL Engineer Trainee vacancies: 250 Electrical, 48 Civil and 21 Computer Science</h2>
<p>The vacancy notice gives a discipline-wise split of the 319 posts: Engineer Trainee (Electrical) carries 250 posts, Engineer Trainee (Civil) carries 48 posts, and Engineer Trainee (Computer Science) carries 21 posts. The notice does not print a category-wise split across General, OBC, SC, ST, EWS and PwBD, and it directs candidates to the careers section of the POWERGRID website for the remaining details. Reservation follows Government of India directives, as the original advertisement states.</p>
<p>Electronics candidates need to read the notice with care. The original advertisement of 27 September 2025 listed disciplines tentatively, but the vacancy notice of 3 November 2026 lists no Electronics vacancies. Electronics graduates should check the careers section on powergrid.in before planning an application, rather than assuming a post exists for their branch.</p>
<h2>PGCIL Engineer Trainee eligibility: GATE 2026 score, 60 per cent marks and age 28</h2>
<p>Applicants need a full-time B.E., B.Tech. or B.Sc. (Engineering) degree from a recognised university or institute, with at least 60 per cent marks or an equivalent CGPA, plus a valid GATE 2026 score in the matching paper. Electrical posts require the EE paper, Civil posts require the CE paper, and Computer Science posts require the CS paper. For SC, ST and PwBD candidates, the qualifying marks are relaxed to pass marks on posts reserved for their category. Only a GATE 2026 score counts: a GATE 2025 or earlier score is not valid. Only Indian nationals aged 18 or above are eligible.</p>
<p>The upper age limit is 28 years as on 31 December 2025. Relaxation applies on posts reserved for the category, as per Government of India directives: 3 years for OBC (NCL), 5 years for SC and ST, and 10, 13 or 15 years for PwBD candidates depending on category. Final-year or final-semester students who expected their results by 14 August 2026 could apply if they held at least 60 per cent up to the pre-final examination, with selection subject to securing 60 per cent in the final qualification. Candidates working in a government organisation or public sector undertaking must produce a No Objection Certificate at the interview stage.</p>
<h2>PGCIL Engineer Trainee salary and service bond</h2>
<p>During the one-year training period, trainees draw the scale of Rs. 40,000 to Rs. 1,40,000 (IDA), with basic pay of Rs. 40,000 plus IDA, HRA and perks at 12 per cent of basic, giving an approximate CTC of Rs. 11 lakh a year. After training, the post becomes Engineer in the E2 scale of Rs. 50,000 to Rs. 1,60,000 (IDA), with an approximate CTC of Rs. 22.5 lakh a year. The CTC varies with the place of posting, and regular employees also receive performance related pay, company leased accommodation or HRA, conveyance reimbursement, a laptop facility, provident fund, gratuity, pension, group insurance and medical facilities for self and dependants.</p>
<p>There is a service bond to weigh before applying. Selected candidates must complete the training and serve for at least 3 years, with a bond of Rs. 5,00,000 for General, OBC (NCL) and EWS candidates and Rs. 2,50,000 for SC, ST and PwBD candidates. Graduates who want to pursue further studies on the basis of GATE 2026, or who need extra time to join, should not apply, the advertisement states.</p>
<h2>PGCIL Engineer Trainee selection: GATE 2026 carries 85 per cent weight</h2>
<p>There is no separate written examination. Selection rests on the GATE 2026 score, carrying 85 per cent weight, followed by a Group Discussion carrying 3 per cent and a Personal Interview carrying 12 per cent, with a pre-employment medical examination for selected candidates. The interview carries minimum qualifying marks of 40 per cent for Unreserved and EWS candidates and 30 per cent for OBC (NCL), SC, ST and PwBD candidates on posts reserved for them. The Group Discussion and Interview can be held in Hindi or English, as the candidate chooses. If marks are equal, the candidate elder by date of birth is ranked first, and mere appearance in GATE 2026 or submission of an application does not guarantee further consideration.</p>
<h2>How to apply for the PGCIL Engineer Trainee posts</h2>
<p>First, keep the GATE 2026 application number and registration number ready, along with category certificates where applicable. Second, visit powergrid.in and open the careers section, where the vacancy notice directs candidates to register online. Third, register with the GATE 2026 details and fill in the personal and educational details. Fourth, pay the Rs. 500 fee online through the candidate login, if not exempt, since the application is complete only after the fee is paid. Fifth, submit the form before 11:59 PM on 18 November 2026 and keep the GATE 2026 admit card and score card safe for verification later. The portal can slow down on the last evening, so waiting until then is a poor plan.</p>
<p>For readers in Nepal, the limits need to be plain. POWERGRID is an Indian public sector undertaking, and the advertisement states that only Indian nationals are eligible, so readers outside India should check the official notification's eligibility conditions before applying rather than assuming a foreign application will be accepted. No agent can secure a POWERGRID post, and anyone asking for a fee to arrange one should not be trusted. Apply only through powergrid.in. Growentix also covers other Indian government engineering recruitment, including <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/appsc-assistant-executive-engineer-503-posts-20261103">APPSC Assistant Executive Engineer recruitment for 503 posts</a> and <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/bel-probationary-engineer-350-posts-20261028">BEL Probationary Engineer recruitment for 350 posts</a>.</p>
<p><small>Sources: POWERGRID <a href="https://www.powergrid.in/en/job-opportunities/recruitment-engineer-trainees-2026-through-gate-2026-advt-no-cc042025-dated">recruitment page for Engineer Trainees 2026</a>, and the GovtJobsAlert report on the vacancy notice dated 3 November 2026.</small></p>$$,
'',
ARRAY['https://www.powergrid.in/en/job-opportunities/recruitment-engineer-trainees-2026-through-gate-2026-advt-no-cc042025-dated', 'https://govtjobsalert.in/pgcil-engineer-trainee-recruitment-2026/'],
NOW(), NOW(), NOW())
ON CONFLICT ("slug") DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'supreme-court-court-assistant-programmer-15-posts-20261103', 'global', 'India',
'Supreme Court Court Assistant Recruitment 2026: 15 Programmer Posts, Apply by 24 November',
'',
'The Supreme Court of India Registry opened applications on 3 November 2026 for 15 tentative Court Assistant (Technical Assistant-cum-Programmer) posts at Level 7 pay; the window closes on 24 November 2026 at 11:55 PM.',
'',
$$<p>Supreme Court Court Assistant recruitment 2026 opened on 3 November 2026, when the Registry of the Supreme Court of India invited online applications for 15 tentative vacancies of Court Assistant (Technical Assistant-cum-Programmer). The post is a Group B, non-gazetted post at Level 7 of the Pay Matrix, carrying Rs. 44,900 to Rs. 1,42,400 with an initial basic pay of Rs. 44,900 plus allowances. Applications close on 24 November 2026 at 11:55 PM, and the advertisement, No. F.6/2026/Tech.Post/SC(RC), is dated 12 October 2026. Only Indian citizens can apply, and this is not a fresher post: every route demands years of software development experience.</p>
<h2>At a glance</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Organisation</th><td>Supreme Court of India (Registry)</td></tr>
<tr><th>Post</th><td>Court Assistant (Technical Assistant-cum-Programmer), Group B, non-gazetted</td></tr>
<tr><th>Advertisement number</th><td>F.6/2026/Tech.Post/SC(RC), dated 12 October 2026</td></tr>
<tr><th>Total vacancies</th><td>15 (tentative)</td></tr>
<tr><th>Pay</th><td>Level 7, Rs. 44,900 to Rs. 1,42,400, initial basic pay Rs. 44,900 plus allowances</td></tr>
<tr><th>Application window</th><td>3 November 2026 to 24 November 2026, 11:55 PM</td></tr>
<tr><th>Application mode</th><td>Online only, through sci.gov.in</td></tr>
<tr><th>Application fee</th><td>Rs. 1,500 for General and Unreserved; Rs. 750 for SC, ST, OBC (Non-Creamy Layer), ex-servicemen and physically challenged candidates, plus bank charges</td></tr>
<tr><th>Objective tests</th><td>Tentatively 22 December 2026</td></tr>
<tr><th>Test centres</th><td>Delhi/NCR, Mumbai, Kolkata, Hyderabad and Bengaluru</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>Supreme Court Court Assistant vacancies: 15 tentative posts for a panel</h2>
<p>The 15 posts are tentative and may change because of administrative needs, and the recruitment is held to prepare a panel. The advertisement gives no category-wise split; reservation follows Rule 4A of the Supreme Court Officers and Servants (Conditions of Service and Conduct) Rules, 1961. Applicants should therefore not rely on a category-wise figure read elsewhere, and should check the Registry's own updates for any change to the vacancy count.</p>
<p>This post should not be confused with other Supreme Court recruitment running in parallel. The Court Assistant (Technical Assistant-cum-Programmer) post is a technical programming post with an experience requirement, and it is distinct from non-technical court posts advertised separately. Applying for the wrong post, or assuming the eligibility of one post applies to another, is an easy mistake to make when several Registry advertisements are open at once.</p>
<h2>Supreme Court Court Assistant eligibility: a computer degree plus software experience</h2>
<p>Qualifications and age are counted as on 1 October 2026, and only Indian citizens can apply. Candidates must meet one of three routes. The first route is a B.E. or B.Tech. in Computer Science or Information Technology from a recognised university, plus 3 years of experience in software development. The second route is a Master's degree in Computer Application or M.Sc. in Computer Science, plus 3 years of software development experience. The third route is a B.Sc. in Computer Science or a Bachelor in Computer Application with first class or at least 60 per cent aggregate, plus 4 years of software development experience. All degrees must be from a recognised university or equivalent, the experience must be in software development, and experience certificates are uploaded with the form and checked again at document verification. A law degree is preferred and counts as an additional qualification, but it is not compulsory.</p>
<p>The age limit is 18 to 35 years as on 1 October 2026, with usual relaxation as per Government of India rules for SC, ST, OBC (Non-Creamy Layer), physically challenged and ex-servicemen candidates; the advertisement does not state the relaxation years. Dependants of freedom fighters receive up to 5 years over the limit, and departmental candidates of the Registry face no upper age limit. Staff of other government departments, public sector undertakings and nationalised banks receive no age relaxation and must submit a No Objection Certificate from their employer at document verification. Counting experience years carefully against the cut-off date of 1 October 2026 is essential, since a shortfall discovered at verification ends the candidature.</p>
<h2>Supreme Court Court Assistant selection: two objective tests, a practical test and an interview</h2>
<p>Selection has four stages. The Objective Type Test runs for 1.5 hours, carries 100 marks and needs a minimum of 50 marks to qualify. The Objective Type Technical Aptitude Test also runs for 1.5 hours, carries 50 marks and needs a minimum of 25 marks. The Practical Aptitude Test runs for 1.5 hours, carries 50 marks and needs a minimum of 25 marks. The interview carries 25 marks and needs a minimum of 13 marks. Both objective tests carry negative marking of 0.25 marks per wrong answer, so leaving a question blank when truly unsure is wiser than guessing.</p>
<p>The first test contains 100 questions: 40 on General English including comprehension, 30 on General Aptitude and 30 on Reasoning and Quantitative Aptitude. If many candidates qualify by scoring at least 50 per cent in each objective test, the top candidates in a 1:10 ratio against the tentative vacancies are shortlisted for the Practical Aptitude Test, and candidates who qualify all three tests are called for interview in a 1:3 ratio. The two objective tests are tentatively scheduled for 22 December 2026 in Delhi/NCR, Mumbai, Kolkata, Hyderabad and Bengaluru, with cities chosen in order of preference; the Practical Aptitude Test is held in Delhi/NCR, and the interview is held online or in Delhi, as decided. Answer keys are due to be uploaded on 24 December 2026, with an objection window from 24 December 2026 to 25 December 2026 at 11:55 PM, at Rs. 100 per objection, refunded if the objection is found valid. Admission at every stage is purely provisional until documents are verified, and the Registry reserves the right to change the selection process without notice.</p>
<h2>How to apply for the Supreme Court Court Assistant posts</h2>
<p>First, from 3 November 2026, open the recruitment link on the Supreme Court website, sci.gov.in. Second, fill in the online application form with details exactly as on the Class X certificate, and give the undertaking that the advertisement has been read and will be followed. Third, upload the photograph, signature, Class X, Class XII, graduation and experience certificates, plus caste or disability certificate if applicable. Fourth, pay the fee online through the UCO Bank payment gateway; the fee is non-refundable, bank charges are extra, and no postal application is accepted. Fifth, preview, submit and keep the application number for the admit card, which is downloaded from the website and not sent by post.</p>
<p>Three conditions deserve emphasis. If more than one form is submitted, only the last one is considered, and a submitted form cannot be withdrawn or modified, with no change of category allowed. Printouts need not be sent to the Registry. And a personal email address and mobile number that stay active must be used, because test dates and updates are sent by SMS and email. No travelling allowance is paid for tests or interview, and a change of centre or date is not entertained. For readers in Nepal, the citizenship limit is the first fact to settle: the advertisement states that only Indian citizens can apply, so readers outside India should not assume eligibility. No agent can secure a Registry post, and applications go only through sci.gov.in. Growentix also covers other Indian government recruitment, including <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/appsc-assistant-executive-engineer-503-posts-20261103">APPSC Assistant Executive Engineer recruitment for 503 posts</a> and <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/bel-probationary-engineer-350-posts-20261028">BEL Probationary Engineer recruitment for 350 posts</a>.</p>
<p><small>Sources: Supreme Court of India, sci.gov.in, and the GovtJobsAlert report on Advertisement No. F.6/2026/Tech.Post/SC(RC).</small></p>$$,
'',
ARRAY['https://www.sci.gov.in/', 'https://govtjobsalert.in/supreme-court-court-assistant-programmer-recruitment-2026/'],
NOW(), NOW(), NOW())
ON CONFLICT ("slug") DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'ai-jobs-india-hiring-october-naukri-jobspeak-20261103', 'global', 'India',
'AI Jobs in India Stay Strong as White-Collar Hiring Rises 14% in October, Naukri Data Shows',
'',
'Naukri''s JobSpeak index rose to 2,836 in October 2026 from 2,480 in October 2025, a 14 per cent year-on-year rise, with demand for AI skills remaining robust, the jobs site said in a report published on 3 November 2026.',
'',
$$<p>AI jobs in India stayed in demand through October 2026, even as hiring growth was led by factory and production roles. White-collar hiring in India rose 14 per cent year-on-year in October, while hiring for artificial intelligence (AI) skills and senior professionals also remained robust, according to a report from jobs site Naukri published on 3 November 2026. The report, based on the Naukri JobSpeak index, put the index at 2,836 for October 2026, up from 2,480 in October 2025. The figures describe hiring activity across the platform, not a single employer's vacancies, and they give jobseekers a clearer read on where demand actually sits than any one job advertisement can.</p>
<h2>At a glance</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Report</th><td>Naukri JobSpeak, October 2026</td></tr>
<tr><th>Report published</th><td>3 November 2026</td></tr>
<tr><th>JobSpeak index, October 2026</th><td>2,836</td></tr>
<tr><th>JobSpeak index, October 2025</th><td>2,480</td></tr>
<tr><th>Year-on-year change</th><td>Up 14 per cent</td></tr>
<tr><th>Strongest sector growth</th><td>Automobile, up 36 per cent</td></tr>
<tr><th>Other strong sectors</th><td>BPO/ITES, up 17 per cent; banking and financial services, up 16 per cent</td></tr>
<tr><th>Fastest-rising job function</th><td>Production and manufacturing, up 39 per cent</td></tr>
<tr><th>Fresher recruitment</th><td>Up 15 per cent</td></tr>
<tr><th>Senior hiring (13 to 16 years, and over 16 years)</th><td>Up 22 per cent in each band</td></tr>
<tr><th>Vacancies announced in this report</th><td>None; this is a hiring trends report, not a recruitment notice</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>AI jobs in India: demand for AI skills remains robust</h2>
<p>The headline for AI jobseekers is a single sentence in the report: demand for AI skills remains strong. Hitesh Oberoi, Managing Director and Chief Executive Officer of Info Edge (India), the parent company of Naukri, said manufacturing companies are building capacity, and demand for AI skills remains strong, which he described as indicative of the selective, fundamentals-led nature of hiring demand. In plain terms, employers are hiring for skills they can tie to output, rather than hiring broadly. AI skills sit in that first group, alongside production and manufacturing skills, while sales hiring grew far more slowly.</p>
<p>That selectivity cuts both ways for applicants. A candidate with demonstrable AI work stands in a market where demand is holding up, but the report gives no AI-specific vacancy count, salary figure or list of hiring companies. The honest reading is that AI skills help a candidate get shortlisted; they do not guarantee a role, and the report alone cannot tell any individual whether a particular employer is hiring. The same caution applies to the wider AI hiring picture: a fresh hub announcement, such as the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/slalom-singapore-hub-ai-jobs-20261103">Slalom Singapore hub with AI jobs across Cloud, Data and Engineering</a>, names hiring teams but no headcount, while survey evidence from South Korea shows <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-contact-centre-jobs-south-korea-redesign-20261103">AI contact centre jobs being redesigned rather than cut</a>. Trends point to where to look; only a live posting defines a real opening.</p>
<h2>Where hiring grew fastest in October: automobiles, production and manufacturing</h2>
<p>The automobile sector recorded the strongest hiring growth at 36 per cent. Within automobiles, hiring for production and manufacturing roles rose 41 per cent, research and development rose 53 per cent, and procurement and supply chain rose 50 per cent, while sales and business development grew only 7 per cent. Auto companies are adding people to design, source and build vehicles at a much faster rate than they are adding people to sell them, and that pattern repeats across the report.</p>
<p>Across industries, hiring for production and manufacturing functions grew 39 per cent, making it one of the fastest-rising job functions. In industrial products and heavy machinery, production hiring grew 40 per cent and quality assurance 41 per cent. In construction, engineering, cement and metals, production roles rose 33 per cent, project and programme management rose 25 per cent, and site engineering rose 20 per cent. The FMCG sector grew a modest 6 per cent overall, yet hiring for its production functions rose 40 per cent and procurement 20 per cent, while sales and business development hiring in FMCG fell 5 per cent. Banking and financial services grew 16 per cent, and BPO/ITES grew 17 per cent, rounding out a month in which building, making and operating outpaced selling.</p>
<h2>Experience bands: freshers up 15 per cent, senior hiring up 22 per cent</h2>
<p>Hiring for professionals with 13 to 16 years of experience grew 22 per cent, and hiring for professionals with over 16 years of experience also grew 22 per cent. Fresher recruitment rose 15 per cent, while the 8 to 12 years band grew 14 per cent and the 4 to 7 years band grew 11 per cent. The spread matters for applicants at different career stages: the strongest percentage growth sat with the most experienced professionals, but fresher hiring also grew at a healthy rate, so the market was not closed to new entrants.</p>
<p>One qualification in the report should not be skipped. Part of the increase reflected a favourable base effect, the report noted, because Diwali fell in October 2025, weighing on recruitment activity during the corresponding period last year. A year-on-year comparison against a festival-affected month will always look stronger than a comparison against a normal month, so the 14 per cent figure should be read as a genuine rise with a flattering base, not as proof that every sector is booming. Readers comparing months should keep that base effect in mind before drawing conclusions about any single industry.</p>
<h2>What to do next</h2>
<p>First, be clear about what this story is not. It announces no vacancies, no employer hiring drive and no application route, so it cannot be applied to. Anyone who wants an AI role should look for actual postings from named employers, and should check each posting's stated eligibility, location and deadline rather than treating a national trend as a personal opening.</p>
<p>Second, match preparation to the two things the report rewards: demonstrable skill and experience. For AI roles, that means shipped work a hiring manager can inspect, not a certificate alone. For production and manufacturing roles, it means the technical and quality skills the growing functions name. For senior roles, it means a record of delivery at scale, since the fastest-growing bands are the most experienced ones.</p>
<p>Third, use the sector pattern to aim applications. Automobile, industrial products, construction and engineering employers are hiring production, research, quality and supply chain staff faster than sales staff, so candidates with those skills face a friendlier market than candidates chasing sales roles in the same sectors. BPO/ITES and banking and financial services also grew, giving service-sector applicants a second lane.</p>
<p>Finally, beware of anyone selling this report as a job offer. No fee, agent or social post can convert a hiring index into employment, and no legitimate employer charges a fee to apply. For readers in Nepal, the same caution applies with an extra step: this report covers hiring in India, and each posting carries its own eligibility rules, so check those rules before applying rather than assuming a posting is open to every nationality. Apply only through the employer's own careers page or a posting that names the employer and links to it.</p>
<p><small>Sources: IANS report on the Naukri JobSpeak October 2026 figures, published 3 November 2026.</small></p>$$,
'',
ARRAY['https://tennews.in/indias-white-collar-hiring-up-14-pc-in-oct-on-manufacturing-push-report/'],
NOW(), NOW(), NOW())
ON CONFLICT ("slug") DO NOTHING;
